ASCILINE-Rust unique-frame throughput benchmark
Grid: 240x135; duration: 4s; source: deterministic ffmpeg testsrc2
A unique hash count equal to source frames prevents duplicated 60fps content from masquerading as 120fps input.

=== target 60 fps ===
INIT: fps=60.0 mode=6 grid=240x135
frames in 5.0s: 240 → 48.0 fps
source frames: 240
unique source hashes: 240
server-sent frames: 240
timestamp-derived wire rate: 61.9 fps

=== target 120 fps ===
live INIT: fps=120 mode=6 grid=240x135 pixel=true -> 480x270
live capture: 480 frames in 4.11s -> 116.9 fps (480x270 per frame)
source frames: 480
unique source hashes: 480
server-sent frames: 480
timestamp-derived wire rate: 124.9 fps

=== target 240 fps ===
INIT: fps=240.0 mode=6 grid=240x135
frames in 5.0s: 960 → 192.0 fps
source frames: 960
unique source hashes: 960
server-sent frames: 960
timestamp-derived wire rate: 249.3 fps

=== target 480 fps ===
INIT: fps=480.0 mode=6 grid=240x135
frames in 5.0s: 1920 → 384.0 fps
source frames: 1920
unique source hashes: 1920
server-sent frames: 1920
timestamp-derived wire rate: 494.7 fps


Interpretation:
- The source has a unique framemd5 for every frame; no duplicated 60fps source is used.
- Server-sent count equals the complete source count at each tested rate.
- `throughput_120fps.mp4` and `.gif` are visual wire captures; their playback rate is not the measurement.
- The timestamp-derived rates are machine measurements, not an unlimited performance guarantee.
